    {
      "id": "ae9b7eb9-b4bf-4a77-bd21-79eacfce6e56",
      "name": "Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -1744,
        -1552
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 1050,
        "height": 742,
        "content": "# Structured AI batches for agents\n\nGive Codex, Claude Code, or another agent an authenticated webhook for repetitive AI work. A script sends independent calls with a system prompt, user message, JSON output schema, and `small` or `big` mode. The workflow processes the first 50 calls and returns structured results in request order.\n\n### How it works\n\n1. **Authenticate:** Check `X-API-Key` before any model call. Unknown keys receive HTTP 401.\n2. **Process:** Use one `aiMode` per request. Split `body.calls` into items; **Limit to 50** processes the first 50 in parallel. Extra calls are dropped without an error.\n3. **Return and trace:** Return one `{query, output}` entry per processed item. **Insert log** stores the user's email and the full submitted calls payload, including prompts, schemas, and mode. Model outputs are not logged.\n\n### Setup\n\n1. Create Data Tables: `users(email: string, auth_token: string)` and `logs(user: string, log: string)`.\n2. Add one row per user with a unique `auth_token`. For this example, generate a unique 12-character alphanumeric code with a cryptographically secure random generator and deliver it privately. Organizations can use their own identity and token system.\n3. In **Check Auth**, select this imported workflow. Use the blue notes to connect both tables and model credentials. **Get team member** looks up `auth_token`.\n4. The log retains submitted prompts and source text. Send only data approved for that retention and restrict access to the logs table.\n\n### Publish and customize\n\nPublish the workflow. Give each user the production URL and their token through a verified private channel. Store them as `EXTERNALIZED_AI_URL=...` and `EXTERNALIZED_AI_API_TOKEN=...` in `.env`, without quotes.\n\nReplace the models or providers while keeping the `small`/`big` contract. Direct OpenRouter calls could add per-call model selection, measured cost, reasoning effort, or Jev."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f17e4585-572e-4ca7-80a4-52cbe1acf50c",
      "name": "Agent skill and onboarding",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -1744,
        -768
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 1050,
        "height": 1242,
        "content": "# Use this for your AI Agent's skill\n\n**Purpose.** Use this endpoint for repetitive, independent AI tasks such as classification, extraction, rewriting, and structured generation. Have the main agent plan once, then use a script to send many similar calls to external models. This can save the main agent's context and time when its full capabilities are unnecessary for each item. Keep deterministic IDs, dates, URLs, and exact quotes in the script.\n\n### Onboard an unconfigured person\n\n1. Check whether `EXTERNALIZED_AI_URL` and `EXTERNALIZED_AI_API_TOKEN` are configured. Report only \u201cpresent\u201d or \u201cmissing\u201d. Never print or log the token value. If either is missing, stop before making a paid request.\n2. Ask the person to request the production webhook URL and a personal token from the workflow owner. Deliver tokens through a verified private channel.\n3. Store them locally as `EXTERNALIZED_AI_URL=...` and `EXTERNALIZED_AI_API_TOKEN=...` in `.env`, without quotes. Keep the file out of version control. Never put the token in this skill, source code, a prompt, or a URL.\n4. Test one cheap, authorized call. If it returns 401, ask the owner to check the token and the `users.auth_token` lookup.\n\n### Build and send a batch\n\nUse one `aiMode` per request. Split `small` and `big` work into separate requests. Send at most 50 calls to `POST $EXTERNALIZED_AI_URL` with `Content-Type: application/json` and `X-API-Key: $EXTERNALIZED_AI_API_TOKEN`. Only the first 50 are processed if a request contains more.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"calls\": [{\n    \"systemPrompt\": \"Shared rules for this item type.\",\n    \"userMessage\": \"Task and source text for one item.\",\n    \"jsonSchema\": \"{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"label\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"}},\\\"required\\\":[\\\"label\\\"]}\",\n    \"aiMode\": \"small\"\n  }]\n}\n```\n\n`jsonSchema` must be a JSON-encoded string, not a nested object. Use `small` for routine bulk work and `big` for the stronger tier. Keep the system prompt shared across similar items; put item-specific content in `userMessage`.\n\n### Logging and sensitive input\n\nThe `logs` table stores the user's email and the full submitted calls payload, including `systemPrompt`, `userMessage`, `jsonSchema`, and `aiMode`. The sub-workflow currently wraps the calls array in a property named `callCount`; that property contains the payload, not a numeric count. Treat all submitted source text and prompts as retained data. Do not send secrets or content the organization does not permit storing. Ask the owner about access and retention settings. Model outputs are not logged.\n\n### Verify results\n\nBuild a dry run that reports the call count and one redacted sample without calling the endpoint. Pilot a few items before the full job. Tell the person the planned count, tier, and estimated cost, or explain why a reliable estimate is unavailable.\n\nThe response is an array in input order. Read `response[i].output` and match it to `calls[i]`. The `query` field echoes the prompt. Treat missing or error outputs as failures, retry only failed items with a limit, and compare submitted and returned counts."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "6c7c8320-8edf-454e-aec4-a8a5ceba5a17",
      "name": "Auth before AI",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -336,
        -1520
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 890,
        "height": 596,
        "content": "## Authentication gate\n\n**Check Auth** sends the API key to the lookup sub-workflow. **Valid?** starts the AI path only after success; the other branch returns HTTP 401. Use the blue note beside **Get team member** to select the table and correct token column."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "5edd35ff-f08a-47a6-8886-702b16ce31e4",
      "name": "Batch and model routing",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        608,
        -1520
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 700,
        "height": 436,
        "content": "## Batch preparation\n\n**Prepare Calls** restores the webhook body; **Split Calls** creates one item per call. **Limit to 50** passes only the first 50. Extra calls are dropped without an error. Callers should split larger jobs and compare request and response counts."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ff937b03-baa6-4c24-aaad-aef52da1747b",
      "name": "Results and logging",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        2144,
        -1520
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 588,
        "height": 436,
        "content": "## Response shape\n\n**Aggregate Responses** collects the processed items. **Respond to Webhook** returns ordered `{query, output}` entries. `query` echoes the prompt; an item can lack usable `output`. Callers check each result, retry failures, and expect at most 50 entries."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "e260e9f3-9062-4bad-9b54-11b2bc6a4b78",
      "name": "Credential lookup and audit",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -544,
        -560
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 1642,
        "height": 782,
        "content": "## Auth and audit sub-workflow\n\n**Get team member** looks up `auth_token`. **Found?** routes matches to **Insert log**, which stores the user's email and serialized calls payload. That payload includes prompts, source text, schemas, and mode, not just a count. Its wrapper is currently named `callCount`, though it contains the calls array. Outputs are not logged."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
